id to boris what  i've been saying for many uh months now  and others around me  that his position is untenable and that  has come to a head at last thank god it  has come to a head because it was  completely distracting government from  getting on with the job of government  which is what we desperately need to get  back to uh  to to doing and so the game is up he  can't replace all those holes in the in  the government with other people because  frankly i think it really  struggled to find people who would now  want to serve in his government because  it's going to be a very short-term  appointment because frankly  he will have to go one way or the other  in the next few days i hope he does the  honourable thing and resigns on his own  terms but if not  then the 1922 committee executive will  change the rules next week and we'll  have another vote of confidence and as  bob seeley said earlier to you i've  absolutely no doubt that there will be a  not insignificant majority now saying  sorry prime minister but we don't have  the confidence in you anymore and if we  would have a very different result to  just a few weeks ago  it's interesting let's unpick that a bit  the 1922 committee who can call a  confidence  motion if enough letters go in but of  course they do need to change the rules  first to allow boris johnson to face a  second confidence motion after he  survived the first one how quickly do  you think this could all happen they're  obviously meeting to elect the new  officials on monday  well it could happen and it needs to  happen very quickly so what the 22  committee executives have decided and  they have a lot of power in this they  run the whole election uh procedures  they've and i think sensibly decided  that given we have elections annual  elections for the 22 executive uh which  is due to happen in the next few weeks  that it should be for the new committee  to decide if there's to be a rule change  because that's a big move and it's not  something i would like to see done uh  lightly so they've brought forward the  elections to monday so on on monday all  the nominations will be in  every conservative mp will have a vote  we will elect a new  uh executive committee of the 1922  and they will then decide whether they  want to change the rules and they'll do  so i think with a very  fresh and credible mandate having just  been elected to those positions so  people can't cry foul to say it was you  know they haven't got a mandate to well  they will have a brand new  mandate and if they decide there needs  to be a new vote of confidence which is  i think they will  then we can vote as early as monday  evening or or tuesday  and if that comes up with a vote of no  confidence of the prime minister's i  very strongly suspect it will then a  leadership challenge and a legit  competition can be put into place very  quickly and it all frankly needs to  happen before the summer recess in just  a few weeks time  before the final two contenders as it  works within a party structure can then  go out for the vote of the membership at  large in the in the country and i think  that process needs to be revisited  because it can take several months we  haven't got several months we need to  get on with this we need to have a new  leader a new prime minister getting  government back in the business of  running government  let's talk about that potential  leadership election shall we because it  did feel as if one of the reasons that  perhaps people  stayed their hands if you like at the  last vote was because there was no clear  challenger to the prime minister do you  think that has changed do you think  there are serious contenders now and is  there anyone that you would particularly  uh point to as having a good uh chance  at getting the top job  well there are serious uh contenders and  it isn't like you know back in the time  mrs thatcher that was michael hesden who  was uh circling uh with great ambitions  to be uh to 